<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_control_system_foundations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Control System Foundations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>To make robots move reliably, engineers use mathematical control frameworks that connect commands to physical behavior. Two foundational categories are open-loop and closed-loop control. This section introduces open-loop systems and explains where they are useful, where they fail, and why they are still widely used in practice.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_open_loop_definition">
    <title>Open-Loop Control Systems</title>
    <p>An open-loop control system computes and applies control signals from pre-defined commands or models without using live sensor feedback to correct motion during execution. For example, if a drivetrain is commanded to run at 50% power for exactly 3 seconds, it will follow that command regardless of slip, friction changes, or obstacles.</p>
    <p>Because the controller does not verify the actual outcome, disturbances can accumulate into large positioning errors.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_open_loop_examples">
    <title>Real-World Open-Loop Examples</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Lawn sprinkler timer:</em> A schedule runs at fixed times and durations without checking soil moisture or rainfall.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Basic conveyor control:</em> Motors run at fixed speed and timing, assuming products move as expected even when jams or slips occur.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_open_loop_usage">
    <title>Why Engineers Still Use Open Loop</title>
    <p>Despite limitations, open-loop control remains common because it is simple, inexpensive, and computationally light. It is often effective when conditions are predictable and tolerance requirements are loose.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Short-duration VEX drivetrain moves in controlled settings</p></li>
      <li><p>Fixed-duration arm or intake actions</p></li>
      <li><p>Constant-speed conveyor operation</p></li>
      <li><p>Preset throttle tests for propulsion systems</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Modern autonomous systems typically require closed-loop feedback to improve robustness and accuracy.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_open_loop_question">
    <title>Concept Check</title>
    <p>A robot drives at fixed motor power for a fixed time and stops short after wheel slip. This is an open-loop failure: the system executed a command but had no feedback mechanism to detect and correct the disturbance.</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
